@* Section V — Professional Law *@
<p>
    This section covers the legislation that creates and governs the professions — worth about
    <strong>10% (7–10 questions)</strong>.
</p>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">Vocabulary</span>
    <p>On the exam, <strong>“assay”</strong> refers to evaluating the characteristics of a substance.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="act"><span class="rref">V.1</span> The Act, Regulations &amp; Bylaws</h2>
<p>
    To regulate professional engineers and geoscientists, each province and territory has enacted the
    <strong>Act</strong> — legislation developed with the Association to protect the public from harm. The Act
    is clarified by the <strong>Regulations</strong>, rules set by the elected Council covering enrolment
    requirements, educational background, ethical behaviour, and misconduct procedures. The Council also
    creates the <strong>Bylaws</strong>, which govern the Association’s own activities — committees, voting
    processes, and charges.
</p>

<h2 id="admission"><span class="rref">V.2</span> Admission to the Professions</h2>
<p>To become a licensed professional (P.Eng. or P.Geo.) you must:</p>
<ul>
    <li>Be a Canadian citizen or equivalent</li>
    <li>Be of good character and reputation</li>
    <li>Be 18 years or older</li>
    <li>Hold an accredited education</li>
    <li>Have at least 4 years of experience, with at least 1 year in Canada</li>
    <li>Demonstrate professional practice and ethics knowledge</li>
    <li>Meet the required English-language proficiency</li>
</ul>
<p>
    For temporary projects, an out-of-province professional may obtain a <strong>Temporary Licence</strong>
    by meeting the qualifications, knowing local regulations, and working with a local Association member
    (which highly skilled individuals may be exempt from). In some provinces, a candidate who meets every
    requirement except the year of Canadian experience may practise under supervision, with all final
    drawings approved by the supervising professional.
</p>
<p>
    Through <strong>reciprocity agreements</strong>, engineers and geoscientists can register with multiple
    Associations — provided they submit the paperwork, have no disciplinary history, permit file-sharing
    between organizations, work toward CPD requirements, and meet language criteria. International agreements
    similarly allow work abroad with mutual recognition of qualifications.
</p>

<h2 id="illegal"><span class="rref">V.3</span> Illegal Practice &amp; Misuse of Title</h2>
<p>
    Unlicensed practice means performing licensed work without approval. Protected terms include
    <strong>P.Eng.</strong> / <strong>P.Geo.</strong>, “Professional Engineer/Geoscientist,” titles such as
    “Software Engineer” or “Oilfield Geoscientist,” and any related abbreviation. It is the Association’s duty
    to enforce the Act; repeated breaches have drawn penalties of <strong>$20,000</strong>.
</p>
<div class="callout">
    <span class="clabel">Important distinction</span>
    <p>
        A graduate cannot call themselves an “Engineer” until registered — only a graduate of an engineering
        program. The Association <strong>enforces</strong> the Act against non-members, while
        <strong>discipline</strong> applies specifically to licensed members.
    </p>
</div>

<h2 id="societies"><span class="rref">V.4</span> Professional &amp; Technical Societies</h2>
<p>
    Associations and societies play different roles. <strong>Associations</strong> govern professional
    conduct; <strong>societies</strong> share engineering knowledge — promoting research, and categorizing
    and disseminating information.
</p>
